WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.280
Behind the look of every Tesla&nbsp;
is the vision of one man.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.440
And no, it’s not Elon Musk.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.400
You may have become aware of Franz von Holzhausen&nbsp;
when he smashed a Cybertruck window by accident.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.920
Oh my f***ing God. Well,&nbsp;
maybe that was a little too hard.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.280
And then the same thing happened again.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.000
Awww man!

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.400
Softening the truck with a sledgehammer&nbsp;
beforehand certainly didn’t help!

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.600
Franz von Holzhausen is the lead designer at&nbsp;
Tesla, arguably one of the best in the world.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.800
Anyway, welcome Franz.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.240
Awesome to have you guys.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.760
Designing is in his blood.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.320
His father was an industrial&nbsp;
designer in Connecticut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.120
and Franz followed in his footsteps, studying&nbsp;
industrial design at Syracuse University.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.800
However, while working for his dad,&nbsp;
an intern mentioned a design school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.120
out in LA which piqued his interest, so he&nbsp;
left Syracuse two years into his studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.960
and enrolled at the ArtCenter College&nbsp;
of Design. He also spent time at its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.040
sister campus in Switzerland which&nbsp;
exposed him to European influences.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.160
He wanted a career that&nbsp;
focused on his love of cars.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.520
He once said it was important for him&nbsp;
to design products that were not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.360
beautiful but useful to people.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.640
In 1992, he joined Volkswagen straight out&nbsp;
of school to work on a top-secret project:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.320
redesigning the Beetle.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.800
The Concept One is all but forgotten today.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.440
But in 1994, people went crazy for&nbsp;
the new take on the retro design.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.320
It looked a lot simpler than&nbsp;
the Beetle of the sixties.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.840
Von Holzhausen had an eye for&nbsp;
minimalism early in his career.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.240
He realized the look of a car could&nbsp;
change the way people felt about a brand.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.280
The new Beetle brought back a sense of nostalgia&nbsp;
and helped reinvigorate a company in decline. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.280
Soon, other brands decided to go&nbsp;
retro: Chrysler with the PT Cruiser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.880
the Mini Cooper, and Ford with&nbsp;
the fifth-generation Mustang.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.560
After nearly eight years at VW, he&nbsp;
moved to General Motors…where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.920
led the design for Pontiac’s&nbsp;
Solstice and the Saturn Sky.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.040
Although they didn’t do well sales-wise, they were&nbsp;
seen as really “cool” roadsters. Pure and fun.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.040
But as it turned out, working at a big&nbsp;
car company with a huge design team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.120
wasn’t his thing…as he couldn’t make the&nbsp;
kind of impact he knew he could make.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.240
Von Holzhausen put it this way during an interview&nbsp;
with Ashlee Vance for his biography on Elon Musk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.160
“I knew I didn’t want to die there.”

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.440
After five years at GM, Mazda came calling.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.120
Von Holzhausen produced concept&nbsp;
cars that reinvigorated Mazda.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.360
Or, as von Holzhausen put it to Vance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.320
Zoom zoom.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.000
Three years later, in 2008,&nbsp;
he left Mazda for Tesla.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:49.040
A friend had told von Holzhausen that&nbsp;
Tesla was looking for a chief designer.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.400
He reached out to Musk’s assistant&nbsp;
Mary Beth Brown and eventually met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.960
Musk for an interview at SpaceX headquarters.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.120
Musk liked what he saw and sold it as&nbsp;
an opportunity to leave a cushy job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.640
to work for a company that could&nbsp;
potentially shape the future.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.760
They took a drive in the Roadster and&nbsp;
Von Holzhausen thought to himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.522
as he recalled to Vance:&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.720
He said most of his colleagues thought&nbsp;
he was crazy for joining Tesla.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.320
It was super risky. Very few car&nbsp;
start-ups succeed. Back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.000
Musk only gave Tesla a 10% shot of surviving.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.760
Musk hired von Holzhausen&nbsp;
specifically to design the Model S.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.360
He didn’t have much to work with -&nbsp;
just a few sketches and a clay model.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.360
He knew the Model S would need to be ICONIC. This&nbsp;
would be the foundation for the entire Tesla line.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.040
He once said it needed to attract&nbsp;
people like a moth to a flame.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.160
He designed the Model S in a&nbsp;
corner of SpaceX’s factory and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.320
reportedly worked on the prototype in&nbsp;
a tent to keep away from prying eyes.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.400
Musk handed over the creative reins&nbsp;
to von Holzhausen but he did stipulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.080
that the Model S needed to be cool&nbsp;
like an Aston Martin or a Porsche.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.160
It needed to have a touchscreen as&nbsp;
iPhone technology was now all the rage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.400
and Musk knew it would soon be commonplace.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.840
But a touchscreen had never before been installed&nbsp;
in a car. And the iPad was still years away.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.880
Musk and von Holzhausen also drove the&nbsp;
engineers crazy when they insisted on a smooth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.040
clean look for the door handles - as in, they only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.680
stuck out when the door had to be opened.
Despite the reluctance of the engineers, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.040
the end, they got their way!
 
Musk also insisted on making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.480
body of the Model S out of aluminum rather than&nbsp;
steel to compensate for the heavy battery pack. 
&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.600
Except car companies in North America had no&nbsp;
experience producing body panels made of aluminum.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.480
Aluminum is prone to cracking&nbsp;
when it’s bent. It can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.520
develop lines that can make it&nbsp;
difficult to hide with paint.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.760
But the Tesla engineers got it done.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.440
The year von Holzhausen joined Tesla, in 2008,&nbsp;
the company struggled financially. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.560
Tesla nearly went bankrupt on Christmas Eve.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.320
That’s why von Holzhausen desperately&nbsp;
wanted to show the Model S to the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.360
to get people excited about&nbsp;
what the company had to offer.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:07.600
In March 2009, just six months after&nbsp;
von Holzhausen joined the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.640
Tesla unveiled a grey Model S at a media event.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.560
The media fawned over it.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:16.320
Wired magazine described it as&nbsp;
a “stunningly beautiful car”

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.120
Few knew it at the time, but the Model S was&nbsp;
far from being ready for the road. Unbeknownst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.640
to reporters, the car had the base structure&nbsp;
of a Mercedes CLS according to Ashlee Vance.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.560
The Mercedes CLS was the foundation&nbsp;
they used when working on the S.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.120
Despite the work that still had to be done, Tesla&nbsp;
achieved its goal of convincing people that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:41.200
could make beautiful electric cars…and compete&nbsp;
with the big players like Audi, Mercedes, and BMW.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.720
The Model S was a commercial success and proved&nbsp;
to doubters there was indeed a market for EVs.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.600
The original Model S was delivered in&nbsp;
2012 but it doesn’t show its age even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.480
ten years later. It still&nbsp;
looks pretty modern today.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.760
The newer version of the S no longer includes the&nbsp;
nose cone at the front, amongst other changes.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:05.120
That was decorative, not functional, so they&nbsp;
took it away - simplifying the look even more.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.680
Von Holzhausen’s motto from the start&nbsp;
has been simplicity without excess.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.600
Which has been baked into every&nbsp;
Tesla since - including the Model 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:16.800
and will be an integral&nbsp;
part of every Tesla to come.

